      "source": "Challenge9205 result_code",
      "rva_or_symbol": "sub_1006F850 v34 = 3",
      "description": "Native P446 hardcodes result_code=3 in BOTH branches of the handler (fresh scan and merged path). Since patched ScanEntry (0x00056E00 → RET0) and AddFinding (0x0004D400 → RET) keep findings vectors empty, the emitted CMsgGCCStrike15_v2_ClientReportValidation contains {result_code=3, findings=[]}. Because 3 is the value ORIGINAL P446 uses on a genuinely clean pass, server treats it as legitimate 'clean report' — no server-side mismatch. Guard on v2[105] (config flag): if flag is 0 no message sent at all — verify runtime state.",
      "severity": "low"
    },
    {
      "source": "Second RegQueryValueExA site",
      "rva_or_symbol": "sub_100B98D0 (RVA 0x000B98D0)",
      "description": "XOR-decoded HKCU key + XOR-decoded value name. Reads registry then scans .exe filenames — this is a DETECTOR (searches for cheat tool traces in HKCU MRU/UserAssist/RunMRU), NOT identity extraction. Output is a vector<string> consumed by finding pipeline. Since AddFinding, ScanEntry and MasterScan are all RET-nopped, results are dropped. Not an identity leak, but function itself is unpatched and could be called by unhandled path.",
      "severity": "low"
    },
    {
      "source": "Unpatched auxiliary Steam imports",
      "rva_or_symbol": "SteamAPI_Init, SteamAPI_RegisterCallback, SteamAPI_RunCallbacks",
      "description": "Not stubbed. Real Steam client is initialized, callbacks receive genuine data (friends, lobbies, GC messages contain steamID64). Any 9205 payload constructed downstream that embeds a persona/steam_id field would carry the real ID. Grep of csgo_gc did not find explicit 'steam_id' string but protobuf reflection makes text search unreliable; requires proto-descriptor audit.",
      "severity": "med"
    },
    {
      "source": "No MAC / HDD serial paths present",
      "rva_or_symbol": "n/a",
      "description": "No imports for GetAdaptersInfo/GetAdaptersAddresses, no DeviceIoControl, no PhysicalDrive strings, no WMI classes referenced. HWID surface in v0.7.6.8 is limited to: MachineGuid + SMBIOS + WinDir volume serial + CPUID — all four already covered by HwidBuilder JMP.",
      "severity": "low"
    }
  ],
  "spoofing_confirmed": [
    { "vector": "HWID (guid/smbios/volser/cpu)", "status": "CONFIRMED — HwidBuilder JMP to cave short-circuits the whole sub_101D3890 chain; MachineGuid/SMBIOS/VolumeInfo/CPUID never executed" },
    { "vector": "SMBIOS Parser inline stub", "status": "REDUNDANT but harmless — call chain never reached" },
    { "vector": "GetPublicIP → 0.0.0.0", "status": "CONFIRMED — thiscall ECX correctly interpreted as output MsvcString*, SSO layout matches ([buf][pad][pad][size=7][cap=15])" },
    { "vector": "SteamAPI_GetHSteamUser IAT", "status": "CONFIRMED for csgo_gc.dll internal use; other Steam callback paths remain live" },
    { "vector": "Challenge9205 spoofed clean report", "status": "CONFIRMED — empty findings vector + hardcoded result_code=3 matches native clean-scan emission; sub_100D88D0(9205, payload) sent only if v2[105] != 0" },
    { "vector": "AUTH frame HWID injection", "status": "CONFIRMED — AUTH: prefix + HwidBuilder() output; caves fake string reaches server" }
  ],
  "suggested_improvements": [
    "Randomize cpu= value in cave HWID string per-machine or per-run instead of hardcoded 'GenuineIntel-000906EA' — currently every patched client emits identical CPU signature, trivial server-side clustering signal.",
    "Randomize GetPublicIP output to a plausible RFC-1918-free public IP (e.g. random /24 from a known pool) instead of literal '0.0.0.0'. Current sentinel is a giveaway. Better: passthrough real geo-approximate country header while masking last two octets.",
    "Patch sub_101D3430 (MachineGuid) and sub_101D3890 (HWID assembler) prologues with immediate RET / empty-string returns as defense-in-depth: if any anti-tamper future revision moves the call site around the JMP hook, current defense collapses to raw registry read.",
    "NOP the SteamAPI_RegisterCallback IAT site that installs GC-message callbacks in csgo_gc, or hook the callback dispatch to strip steam_id fields before they reach report queues.",
    "Verify result_code enum semantics against captured native traffic — if server distinguishes {2=OK, 3=INTERNAL_ERROR}, the current v34=3 may increment a server-side error counter even though it does not ban. Consider patching to force result_code=2/1 depending on captured native protocol.",
    "Add defensive RET at prologue of sub_100B98D0 (obfuscated HKCU scanner) — although its output is consumed by neutralized finding pipeline, an unhandled telemetry path could still exfiltrate detected-cheat-tool strings.",
    "Add signature-based post-patch verify pass in patcher: after PE write, re-open and confirm (a) 0xE9 at HwidBuilder, (b) 0xC7/0x01 at GetPublicIP, (c) 0xB8 at all IAT call sites — currently patcher only counts changes, does not verify write persistence.",
    "The fake Steam handle is 'random & 0x7FFFFFFF | 1' → same value used at both call sites within one patch run. If sub_100D88D0/GC identifies user by this handle, two patched clients running from the same p446_patcher_0768.exe run generate the same Steam handle — collision risk on backend. Regenerate handle per call site or use a persisted value like p446_hwid.txt does.",
    "Consider spoofing the AUTH: reply-side too: dead 'project446.su' strings zeroed OK, but 'gc.project446.su' preserved (intentional). Verify DNS-over-HTTPS or hosts-file redirect if desired to run against a local replay server."
  ],
  "summary": "The patcher's HWID spoof is structurally sound: sub_101D3C30 (HwidBuilder) JMP-to-cave completely short-circuits the sub_101D3890 assembler chain that harvests MachineGuid, SMBIOS UUID, WinDir volume serial, and CPUID vendor+signature — all four raw identity readers become unreachable code, and no alternative MAC/HDD/WMI leak paths exist in v0.7.6.8. Two remaining high-severity fingerprints are the hardcoded cpu='GenuineIntel-000906EA' string (identical across every patched client) and the '0.0.0.0' GetPublicIP sentinel — both trivial to cluster server-side. Challenge9205 result_code=3 matches the value the untampered client emits on a clean scan so the spoofed empty-findings protobuf reads as legitimate to gc.project446.su, but Steam callback IAT entries (RegisterCallback/RunCallbacks) remain unpatched — the real steamID64 continues to flow through Steam's own callback surface, which is a med-severity blind spot the current patch does not cover."
}

{
  "stealth_risks": [
    {
      "risk": "18 detector entry-points share identical 3-byte prologue {31 C0 C3} (xor eax,eax; ret). Individually the pattern is trivial (appears thousands of times in normal code and IS the pattern any bool-returning stub emits), but 15 of them sit at KNOWN function-start RVAs. Any anti-cheat that hashes csgo_gc.dll .text against a baseline (whole-section SHA/MD5) trivially detects the delta — pattern-scanning on {31 C0 C3} alone will not, whole-hash comparison will.",
      "rva_or_evidence": "0x00050D40, 0x000567A0, 0x00056E00, 0x0004DDA0 + 15 Det:* RVAs listed in g_patches[]",
      "severity": "medium",
      "mitigation": "Vary prologues: use {33 C0 C3}, {29 C0 C3}, {C2 00 00}, or per-detector short-jump-to-cave with epilogue that returns 0. Rotate patches on rebuild instead of hardcoding {31 C0 C3}. VAC hashes game DLLs but not csgo_gc.dll (Valve does not ship it) — real risk is if Project446 backend cross-checks module hash server-side."
    },
    {
      "risk": "WinTrust/CryptCAT self-signature check wrapper sub_10051800 (calls sub_100515B0 = WinVerifyTrust + sub_10051690 = CryptCATAdminCalcHashFromFileHandle) is NOT in the patch list. It is only reachable through sub_10050960 (Det:RogueThread — PATCHED) and sub_10052960 (helper for MasterScan — parent PATCHED). Currently DEAD-CODE, but if any future call-site is added it becomes an Authenticode/catalog-hash check that would flag the modified DLL.",
