  "summary": "The patcher's 18 x {31 C0 C3} detector RETs are pattern-safe against naive signature scans (the 3-byte prologue is not distinctive), but a whole-module hash comparison by any external verifier — including Project446's own backend cross-checking msg-9205 findings — would trivially detect the delta; this is the dominant residual risk. No in-DLL BaselineBuilder, no .rdata baseline hash table, no self-referential GetModuleHandleA('csgo_gc.dll') hasher, and no external whitelist/signature-policy file were found; the only unpatched hash-capable primitives are the WinTrust/CryptCAT wrappers (sub_100515B0, sub_10051690, sub_10051800) which are currently dead code (both callers patched) but should be preemptively RET1'd as cheap insurance. The biggest live exposure is the intentionally un-patched Challenge9205 sending a spoofed clean report to gc.project446.su every native re-validation — server-side pattern analysis of persistent zero-finding replies is the one attack surface no in-DLL patch can close."
