{
  "server_detectable_anomalies": [
    {
      "anomaly": "Challenge9205 response latency floor",
      "description": "Challenge9205 handler at sub_1006F850 hardcodes result_code=v34=3 and never actually invokes the anti-tamper scanner (sub_10061680 is only an env-var check for GC_DEBUG_TRUSTED_MODE, not an activation gate). With MonitorLoop RET-patched, no real scan work runs. A legitimate client executes a native re-scan on-challenge (see 'AntiTamper re-scanned on native re-validation') which takes tens to hundreds of ms; our response fires at pure protobuf serialization speed. Server can measure RTT distribution and flag suspiciously flat/low latency.",
      "likelihood": "high"
    },
    {
      "anomaly": "result_code=3 constant regardless of context",
      "description": "v34=3 is stored into field mask 0x200 (proto field 9) unconditionally at the end of Challenge9205 handler. A legit client's result_code is expected to vary based on scan outcome. A server tracking result_code entropy per client_id sees a perfectly deterministic 3 across every audit — trivial statistical fingerprint.",
      "likelihood": "med"
    },
    {
      "anomaly": "Zero-findings on every 9205 response",
      "description": "Because MonitorLoop (sub_10070150, thread spawned at sub_1006FC70) is RET-patched, its queued findings vector is empty at both challenge merge time and periodic flush time (sub_1006FDF0 'native module silent — flushing %d findings standalone'). The report's repeated 'file_hash'/'reason' field stays empty every time. A running CS2 GS with real modules present (client.dll, panorama.dll, tier0.dll) statistically produces occasional benign findings (module unload/reload, DLL rebases, page-guard flips); infinite clean streak is anomalous.",
      "likelihood": "med"
    },
    {
      "anomaly": "No spontaneous 9205 flushes",
      "description": "sub_10070150 (30s tick) and sub_1006FDF0 (native-silent flush) both call sub_100D88D0(this, 9205, ...) unprompted when findings arrive. With MonitorLoop RET, neither ever fires. Server observes a client that only ever responds 9205 as a challenge reply and never volunteers one — differs from a genuine deployment that intermittently reports tamper events (e.g. Steam overlay hook, EAC coexistence tests). If the server keeps a counter of unsolicited 9205 vs challenge-solicited, our ratio is exactly 0.",
      "likelihood": "med"
    },
    {
      "anomaly": "AUTH frame carries no module-hash HMAC",
      "description": "sub_100DC080 builds the AUTH frame from the config bundle serialized at sub_100D93B0 (env vars: SERVER_TOKEN/GC_SERVER_TOKEN, GC_FLEET_SERVER_ID/GC_SERVER_ID, SRCDS_PORT, GC_SERVER_IP). No SHA256/HMAC of csgo_gc.dll/client.dll module bytes is computed into the frame; SHA256/HMAC helpers (0x1054A51C..0x1054E45A, sub_1055B37C) exist in the binary but are not wired into the AUTH path. This is not itself detectable, but it means the server cannot pin a client to a specific module fingerprint at handshake — mitigating one class of tamper detection, but if project446 later ships a server that DOES verify AUTH HMAC, our patched client will fail to compute the expected value.",
      "likelihood": "low"
    },
    {
      "anomaly": "Heartbeat (msg 10002 CMsgServerGcHeartbeat) content still valid",
      "description": "sub_100DA710 sends periodic heartbeat with fields at this+464/468/520..548 (public IP, ports, tick, slot counts). This path is unpatched, so heartbeat itself does not create anomaly. However, one heartbeat field at this+548 is toggled by CommendPlayerQuery (sub_1006F5C0) which cross-references client.dll via GetModuleHandleA and sets byte_106F90C8. If client.dll fails module-integrity check upstream, this field diverges from expected. Low-signal detector.",
      "likelihood": "low"
    }
  ],
  "unhandled_message_types": [
    {
      "msg_id": "9205",
      "description": "CMsgGCCStrike15_v2_ClientReportValidation — HANDLED at sub_1006F850. Not unhandled."
    },
    {
      "msg_id": "9123",
      "description": "CMsgGCCStrike15_v2_ClientCommendPlayerQuery — handled at sub_1006F5C0; also triggers client.dll module presence check."
    },
    {
      "msg_id": "10002",
      "description": "CMsgServerGcHeartbeat — sent by us via sub_100DA710."
    },
    {
      "msg_id": "10021",
      "description": "k_EMsgCustomCraftResult — name-mapped in sub_1009D890; dispatcher default case (falls to loc_10069430)."
    },
    {
      "msg_id": "10022",
      "description": "k_EMsgCustomClearReconnect — name-mapped; default case."
    },
    {
      "msg_id": "9104-9212 default block",
      "description": "Big span (9104-9108, 9110-9111, 9113-9118, 9122, 9124-9130, 9132-9140, 9142-9162, 9165-9190, 9195-9203, 9206-9212) falls to the dispatcher default at jpt_10067DEB / def_10063867. If server ever probes with any of these — including custom project446 audit IDs in that range — client silently drops. That silence itself is a fingerprint if the server issues a decoy audit expecting either an ACK or an error reply."
    }
  ],
  "9205_correctness": {
    "result_code_used": 3,
    "expected": "unknown-server-defined; likely non-3 when findings present. In Challenge9205 (sub_1006F850) the value is hardcoded to 3 and written as field 9 (mask 0x200) after the AntiTamper merge/rescan branches. It is set BEFORE the findings-count sanity check, so a real scan populating findings would still emit result_code=3.",
    "verified": false,
    "notes": "3 is the client-side constant the authors chose; there is no server-side scoring visible in this binary (server logic lives on gc.project446.su). Observed cross-code evidence: (a) sub_1006F850 always assigns v34=3, (b) sub_10070150 and sub_1006FDF0 build the same proto but never overwrite result_code independently, they only append findings. Interpretation is server-side. Given that a normal Valve GC uses result_code as an outcome enum where 0/1 tend to mean 'no reason'/'reason', a constant 3 is a plausible 'passthrough / not scanned' marker — but this is inference, not verification. Recommend passive-capture the actual value real (unmodified) clients send to gc.project446.su and diff; if server-side flags result_code!=X, we are already flagged."
  },
  "summary": "The client's 9205 responses are structurally valid (proto parses, field 9 = 3, empty findings) so no immediate structural detection, but they leak two behavioral fingerprints: near-zero response latency because the on-challenge rescan branch does no real work with MonitorLoop patched, and a perfectly deterministic result_code=3 with an infinite zero-findings streak. The MonitorLoop RET-patch also eliminates every unsolicited 9205 flush (sub_10070150 30s tick and sub_1006FDF0 native-silent flush), so if the project446 server tracks unsolicited-vs-solicited 9205 ratios or expects any non-zero baseline of benign findings, we present a machine-clean profile inconsistent with a real CS2 GS. Everything else — AUTH frame (plain 'AUTH:' + config-derived token, no module HMAC), CMsgServerGcHeartbeat (msg 10002), and CommendPlayerQuery (msg 9123) — behaves normally, so detection risk is concentrated on the 9205 timing/entropy/frequency dimensions rather than on handshake or heartbeat content."
}

